**Summary.** The document fixes the three-layer stance (objects, refs, projection) and states what never crosses between layers. It gives the full AST tuple grammar as a table over the walker's 17 dispatch tags with fixed arity, slot kinds, saturation defaults, and the exclusions from canonical form, each row cited to the walker and codec arms. Addressing is whole-tree `segment-key`, with per-node addresses appearing only in the flat projection and per-lambda subterm addressing declared a follow-up. The segment level references UCF §7.3.2 rather than restating it and defines lowering as a verified pure function between two addresses, recorded as a `:derive` ledger row. Querying covers the flat per-node rows, per-segment `[pc tag & ops]` rows, the verified `q` mechanics with two encoded gotchas, ref-to-code joins, and the three query classes that go to the datom projection. The VM section fixes direct tuple loading as primary, the datom path as projection, both-paths-same-image as conformance, validation as a four-rule grammar checker, and dependency closure as four Datalog queries. The ledger section unifies rulings 6 and 7 as one relation, with the ledger's `[t op address ref]` reading mapped onto datom slots, an op vocabulary extending `dao.datom/reserved`, and a table of all rows including derived-from, macro expansion, and UCF occurrence publication. Migration lists the mechanical sweep with per-file counts and flags the observer lane as unverified. Open items name six blockers or follow-ups without padding.

**Sections:** §1 Stance and layering; §2 The AST tuple grammar (form, slot kinds, table, saturation, exclusions and side tables); §3 The two boundary calls; §4 Addressing (grain, transitional encoder, per-node and per-lambda, sharing replaces `:eid`); §5 The segment level; §6 Querying; §7 The VM around it; §8 The ledger and refs; §9 Migration surface; §10 Acceptance blockers and open items.

**Boundary calls (§3):**

- `:vm/store-update` is excluded from the canonical grammar. The named-primitive variant would add a tag whose meaning is already expressible as an `:application` of `yin/def` over `:vm/store-get`, with the function name profile-checked as an ordinary `:variable`, so it would mint a second address for one program. The node is already outside every persistent form and no test constructs it.
- `:stream/close` exists in both codec and walker, and the walker gains the arm. The codec, linearizer, mnemonic set, semantic hot loop, and engine effect handler all implement it, so removing it would delete working behaviour from the default evaluator to match the non-default one.

One additional call worth flagging: `tail?` is a slot of `:application` only, because the linearizer's `:call` emission is the sole reader of the mark and the yang frontends' marks on other node types are unobservable by both evaluators.

